H=9a453b6720abda445235294c98c6288e616dc5bc1907c78af1755104b3a2e48a; stored-H object_hash=1e9a003077d2afd78e6f14384902f031c6aa140bebaf14702889de13d4cd814a. check succeeds, but run-action a --mut 1 (also --adjoint) and reverse exit 1, empty stdout, §3.3: missing consumed lifetime width. Hand: zero body executions leave d live for the final free; this is identity. infer_widths wrongly removes d while scanning the zero-trip body. Second witness changes bounds to [2], regs bounds {2}, and uses body: d = qalloc any 1; qloop 2 { qfree d; d = qalloc any 2; x d[1]; x d[1] }; qfree d (one instruction per line). H=de76a9295cf8233d97909660d48c9f6ed82abe593735257858457d35a0c0557e; object_hash=128fa2a938dfbdcccdd255aafd128d125737a4a00fb63e9aef791a1f405bfb1a. check and forward execution succeed as identity, but --adjoint and reverse exit 1, empty stdout, §3.3: wire index out of range. The inner free consumes width 1 on iteration 1 and width 2 on iteration 2; inverse execution must recreate width 2 and then width 1. One widths[line]=1 recreates width 1 at both inverse visits, so the next inverse X d[1] fails. Removing the zero-trip wrapper/body from the first witness runs and reverses correctly. No execution budget is involved.

